        <technology>
            <technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>One of the most common ways to apply binders is saturation, which involves the complete immersion of the web or prebonded (needlepunched or thermal bonded) nonwoven either in a binder bath or by flooding the non-woven (Russel, 2007). The amount of binders needed range from 5-60% weight of the nonwoven fabric for impregnation (Khartik, et al., 2016). 

Polyphenylene sulfide fibers can be used for producing heat resistance fabric. In the bonding process. The prebounded web is impregnated with synthetic resins. Preferable thermosetting resins which can be used include epoxy resins (bisphenol A type, bisphenol F type and bisphenol AD type are preferable), phenol resins, vinyl ester resins and unsaturated polyester resins. Among them, bisphenol A type is more preferable for obtaining the shape maintenance of pleats at high temperatures (Kawakami, et al., 1999). To capture the global context, the data provided modelled usage of 5 different resins with equal usage probability. 

The impregnated fabric then dried in stenter frames followed by thermal pressing treatment in hot calendering machine. When the nonwoven is passed through the calendar rollers, it is subjected to an extremely uniform pressure along the entire width of the rollers.

The model represents the process of bonding, hence the input raw materials (Fiber Webs or Prebounded FIber) are not included in the model. The user will use this dataset along with the input raw materials. Energy uses and infrastructure are included in the model.  There is no direct emission to soil and air since the emission comes from electricity usage, while the hazardous waste comes from waste lube oil and heat transfer fluid oil. The hazardous waste is incinerated in this model. The resin is part of the final product. Hence, there is no waste or emission resulted from the usage of resins. In the model, the finishng process is not included in the scope of this dataset and enegy sources of machines used solely from electricity. 

Khartik, T., et al. 2016. Non-woven - Process, Structure, Properties and Applications. New Delhi: Woodhead Publishing India. pp 132 
Kawakami, et al. 1999. Fabric. European Patent Office no EP1022375A1 
Russel, S.J. 2007. Handbook of Nonwovens. England: Woodhead Publishing Limited. pp 158 - 349</technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>
            <technologicalApplicability>na</technologicalApplicability>
